<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollo 8</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 8 was the first crewed spacecraft to leave Earth's gravitational sphere of influence and the first human spaceflight to reach the Moon, flown from 21 to 27 December 1968. Its three astronauts, Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and William Anders, travelled 68 hours to the Moon and orbited it ten times without landing; they were the first humans to see and photograph its far side and an Earthrise. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Eve they read the first ten verses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> in a television broadcast. The mission, the first crewed launch of the Saturn V rocket, ended with a splashdown in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and paved the way for Apollo 11 in July 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollo-8.jpg" alt="Apollo 8" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/Bill Anders · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Apollo 8</b>, flown from 21 to 27 December, was the first human spaceflight to reach the Moon.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
